---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const gameCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'p', label: 'chance the other player cooperates', min: 0, max: 1, step: 0.05, value: 0.5 },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'expected payoff: cooperate', expr: '0.3*p + 0*(1-p)' },
    { label: 'expected payoff: defect', expr: '0.5*p + 0.1*(1-p)' },
    { label: 'defect better when p <', expr: '(0.1-0)/(0.3-0.1+0)' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/advanced/game-theory">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Game theory is <strong>strategy when your outcome depends on others' choices</strong> — and
    theirs on yours. Von Neumann and Morgenstern (1944) formalised it; John Nash (1950, Nobel
    1994) gave it its central concept: the <strong>Nash equilibrium</strong> — a position where
    no player can gain by changing strategy alone, however much everyone could gain by moving
    together.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the prisoner's dilemma, numerically — my best move depends on what I expect from you" widget="sliderLab" config={gameCfg} />

  <p>
    In the dilemma (cooperate pays 3 each; both defect pay 1; exploit pays 5, being exploited 0),
    defection is <em>individually</em> rational at every belief — yet both players defecting is
    worse for both than both cooperating. The gap between individual and collective rationality
    is the theory's deepest lesson: it maps arms races, overfishing, price wars and climate
    negotiations.
  </p>

  <h2>The concepts that travel</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Repeated games change everything:</strong> Axelrod's tournaments (1980) showed tit-for-tat — cooperate first, then mirror — wins long-run iterated dilemmas. Reputation is game-theoretic capital.</li>
    <li><strong>Zero-sum vs positive-sum:</strong> poker is zero-sum; most economics is not. Trade, specialisation and cooperation create value — the possibility the whole subject studies.</li>
    <li><strong>Mechanism design — "game theory in reverse":</strong> design the rules so self-interest produces the outcome you want. Auction design (Nobel 2020, Milgrom & Wilson) allocates radio spectrum worth billions; matching algorithms pair kidney donors and medical students.</li>
    <li><strong>Applications everywhere:</strong> oligopoly pricing, bidding, evolutionary biology (evolutionarily stable strategies), voting paradoxes, and the Cold War's mutually-assured-destruction logic (Schelling, Nobel 2005).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> von Neumann & Morgenstern (1944); Nash, PNAS (1950); Axelrod, The Evolution of Cooperation (1984); Schelling, The Strategy of Conflict (1960).</p>
</TopicPage>
